{
  "section": "interview-questions",
  "slug": "photo-sharing",
  "group": "medium",
  "no": "QUESTION // 06",
  "tag": "MEDIUM",
  "date": "6 Nov 2026",
  "title": "Design photo sharing",
  "dek": "Zotato Snaps lets diners post photos of what they ate. Each one shows on their profile and on the restaurant's page within seconds, loads fast on a 4G phone, and survives a dish going viral.",
  "body": [
    {
      "t": "tldr",
      "items": [
        "Photos are big and written once; their details (who, where, when) are small and read a lot. Bytes go to object storage behind a CDN, details go to a database.",
        "Phones upload straight to object storage with a short-lived signed URL. A queue then hands each photo to workers that make the sizes the app shows.",
        "Nobody downloads the original: the app asks for the size it needs, and the CDN serves almost all of them.",
        "Store details by owner, and keep a second table by restaurant, because both pages list photos newest first.",
        "A viral photo's like count is one hot row. Spread it over several rows, or count in memory and save every few seconds."
      ]
    },
    {
      "t": "example"
    },
    {
      "t": "h2",
      "x": "1. Requirements"
    },
    {
      "t": "list",
      "items": [
        "Post a photo with a caption, tagged with the restaurant and dish.",
        "See a person's photos, and a restaurant's photos, newest first, page by page.",
        "Like a photo and see how many people liked it.",
        "Delete your own photo; it disappears everywhere.",
        "Out of scope here: the home feed of people you follow (that's the News feed question, 07), comments, and video."
      ]
    },
    {
      "t": "table",
      "label": "Qualities to aim for",
      "head": [
        "Quality",
        "Target"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "Speed",
          "A grid of photos loads in about a second on a 4G phone"
        ],
        [
          "Freshness",
          "A new photo shows on the restaurant's page within a few seconds"
        ],
        [
          "Durability",
          "A posted photo is never lost"
        ],
        [
          "Scale",
          "1 crore daily users, 20 lakh new photos a day"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "t": "h2",
      "x": "2. The numbers that matter"
    },
    {
      "t": "list",
      "items": [
        "Uploads: 20 lakh a day ÷ 86,400 s ≈ 23 a second, maybe 100 at the dinner peak. Small for the database.",
        "Bytes in: about 3 MB per original is 6 TB a day, roughly 2 PB a year. Object storage, not a database.",
        "Views: 1 crore people × 50 photos seen is 50 crore a day, about 6,000 a second and 25,000 at peak. At around 100 KB each that's 2.5 GB a second: a CDN's job.",
        "Details: 20 lakh rows a day × about 500 bytes is 1 GB a day, about 365 GB a year. One large database for a while, sharded later.",
        "Sizes: a 20 KB thumbnail, a 150 KB feed size and a 500 KB full screen add about 0.7 MB per photo, so storing them costs a quarter of the originals but saves every phone from downloading 3 MB."
      ]
    },
    {
      "t": "h2",
      "x": "3. Entities and API"
    },
    {
      "t": "list",
      "items": [
        "Photo: `id`, `owner_id`, `restaurant_id`, `dish`, `caption`, `created_at`, `state` (processing, live, removed), `sizes` (keys in object storage).",
        "Restaurant photo: `restaurant_id`, `created_at`, `photo_id` (the same photos, listed by restaurant).",
        "Like: `photo_id`, `user_id`. Like count: `photo_id`, `n`."
      ]
    },
    {
      "t": "code",
      "label": "API",
      "x": "POST /photos   { \"restaurant\": \"r_88\", \"dish\": \"Ghee roast dosa\", \"caption\": \"…\" }\n→ 201 { \"photo\": \"p_7f\", \"upload_url\": \"<signed PUT, 10 min>\" }\n\nPUT <upload_url>            (the image goes straight to object storage)\n\nGET /users/u_12/photos?cursor=…        → 200 { \"photos\": [ { \"id\": \"p_7f\", \"thumb\": \"https://img.zot…/p_7f/320.webp\", … } ], \"cursor\": \"…\" }\nGET /restaurants/r_88/photos?cursor=…  → same shape\nPUT /photos/p_7f/like   DELETE /photos/p_7f/like\nDELETE /photos/p_7f",
      "say": "Posting a photo creates its record and returns a signed upload URL; the app puts the image straight into object storage. A person's photos and a restaurant's photos are both listed newest first with a cursor, each with links to its sizes on the image CDN. Liking is a put or a delete on the photo's like, and the owner can delete a photo."
    },
    {
      "t": "h2",
      "x": "4. High-level design"
    },
    {
      "t": "fig",
      "id": "photo-upload",
      "cap": "The app gets a signed URL and uploads the original straight to object storage. Storage drops a message on a queue; a worker makes three sizes, saves them, and marks the photo live. Phones then fetch the size they need through the CDN, which reads from storage only on a miss."
    },
    {
      "t": "p",
      "x": "The photo service never touches image bytes. It writes the record (state: processing), hands out the upload URL, and later flips the state to live when the resize worker reports back. Until then the owner sees their own photo with a \"processing\" badge, and nobody else sees it. A queue can deliver the same message twice, so the worker must be safe to run again: it writes each size to a fixed key, and marks the photo live only when every size exists and the content check has passed."
    },
    {
      "t": "h2",
      "x": "5. Deep dives"
    },
    {
      "t": "h2",
      "x": "Serving images fast"
    },
    {
      "t": "type",
      "name": "First try",
      "kind": "SERVE THE ORIGINAL",
      "what": "Store the uploaded file and send it to every viewer.",
      "use": "Nothing to build: one file per photo.",
      "avoid": "A grid of 12 photos is 36 MB on a phone. Slow, expensive data for the customer, and the servers send every byte.",
      "ex": "A restaurant page with 12 dish photos takes 30 seconds on patchy 4G.",
      "like": "Mailing someone a poster when they asked for a postcard."
    },
    {
      "t": "type",
      "name": "Better",
      "kind": "SIZES MADE ON UPLOAD",
      "what": "A worker makes fixed sizes (320, 1080 and 2048 pixels wide) in a modern format, and the app asks for the one it needs.",
      "use": "A grid of thumbnails is about 250 KB, and the sizes are made once, not on every view.",
      "avoid": "Served from your own servers, 2.5 GB a second at peak still needs a lot of machines and bandwidth.",
      "ex": "The restaurant grid uses 320-pixel thumbnails at about 20 KB each.",
      "like": "A printer making postcards, posters and stamps once, then handing out the right one."
    },
    {
      "t": "type",
      "name": "Best",
      "kind": "SIZES BEHIND A CDN",
      "what": "Put the sizes behind a CDN with a long cache time. Give every version of a photo its own address that is never reused (the photo id and a version in the path), so it can be cached for a year without ever going stale.",
      "use": "Most views are answered from a server in the viewer's city; storage sees only misses.",
      "avoid": "Deleting a photo must also remove it from the CDN (a purge), or it lingers in caches for a while.",
      "ex": "A dosa photo shared in a Bengaluru food group is fetched once from storage and then served from the Bengaluru edge for everyone else.",
      "like": "A kirana shop stocking the popular items instead of everyone going to the warehouse."
    },
    {
      "t": "h2",
      "x": "Listing photos by person and by restaurant"
    },
    {
      "t": "list",
      "items": [
        "Shard the photo table by owner, so a person's photos are in one place, sorted by `created_at`.",
        "A restaurant's photos come from many owners, so keep a separate table keyed by `restaurant_id` with `created_at` and `photo_id`, written when a photo goes live.",
        "Page with a cursor (the last `created_at` and `photo_id` seen), not page numbers: new photos arriving don't shift what comes next.",
        "The two tables can disagree for a moment. Write the restaurant row from the same event that marks the photo live, and retry until it's there."
      ]
    },
    {
      "t": "h2",
      "x": "A like count for a viral photo"
    },
    {
      "t": "fig",
      "id": "like-counter",
      "cap": "One row taking every like for a viral photo becomes a queue of writers. Split the count over 8 rows: each like adds 1 to a random one, and reading the count adds the 8 up. Each row now takes an eighth of the writes."
    },
    {
      "t": "list",
      "items": [
        "A photo shared in a big group can get 50,000 likes in a few minutes, a few hundred writes a second to one row. Each waits for the one before.",
        "Keep who liked what in the likes table (one row per person, so a double tap doesn't count twice), and the number separately.",
        "Split the number over a few rows and add them up when reading, or count likes in memory and save the total every few seconds. Show the count from a cache; nobody minds if it's a second behind."
      ]
    },
    {
      "t": "h2",
      "x": "Before a photo goes public"
    },
    {
      "t": "list",
      "items": [
        "The resize worker also checks the image: an automated scan for unsafe content, and a person reviews anything it flags.",
        "Remove location data before making the sizes; people don't always know their phone adds it. Keep originals in a private bucket the CDN can't reach, since they still carry it.",
        "A deleted photo is marked removed at once, its by-restaurant row deleted, purged from the CDN, and its files deleted after a few days, in case the delete was a mistake."
      ]
    },
    {
      "t": "h2",
      "x": "What's expected at each level"
    },
    {
      "t": "list",
      "items": [
        "Junior / mid: object storage for images, a database for details, sizes for different screens, and a CDN.",
        "Senior: direct upload with signed URLs, a queue and resize workers, the processing state, a second table by restaurant, cursor pages.",
        "Lead: the hot like count, moderation and privacy (location data), deletion through the CDN, and what happens when resizing falls behind at dinner."
      ]
    },
    {
      "t": "more",
      "label": "Follow-ups an interviewer may ask",
      "body": [
        {
          "t": "list",
          "items": [
            "\"Resize workers fall behind at dinner.\" The queue absorbs it; the owner sees \"processing\" a little longer. Add workers based on queue length, and make the thumbnail first so the grid can show something.",
            "\"Upload fails halfway on 4G.\" Large files go up in parts and resume; a photo that never finished stays in processing and is cleaned up after a day.",
            "\"Same photo uploaded twice?\" Hash the bytes and warn the user; don't silently merge two people's posts.",
            "\"A new screen size needs a 720-pixel version.\" Make it on first request at the CDN's origin and keep it, instead of reprocessing 2 PB of originals.",
            "\"How do you shard when one restaurant has a million photos?\" Its rows are just more rows in the by-restaurant table; split that table by restaurant and time if one restaurant gets too big."
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "t": "lab",
      "x": "Serving photos is a CDN problem at heart. The IND vs PAK challenge shows how much a CDN takes off your servers when everyone wants the same thing at once.",
      "to": "lab/ind-vs-pak",
      "go": "Try the IND vs PAK challenge"
    }
  ],
